\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Proper Spin projections and anisotropic compactness}

\p{Let #{K} be a local field of characteristic different from two as in
\ref{fcap-0044}, and let #{Q} be a nondegenerate quadratic form on a
finite-dimensional #{K}-space #{V}. Use the canonical topologies of
\ref{fcap-0044}. A continuous map is called \newvocab{proper} here if the
inverse image of every compact set is compact. The form #{Q} is
\newvocab{anisotropic} if #{Q(v)=0} implies #{v=0}; otherwise it is
isotropic. This includes the zero-dimensional form as anisotropic,
as in \citet{I.3, Definition 3.1, p. 9}{lam2005quadratic}.}

\p{The Spin projection #{\rho:\Spin(Q)\to SO(Q)} is proper, and

##{\begin{gathered}
\Spin(Q)\text{ is compact}\\
\Longleftrightarrow\quad Q\text{ is anisotropic}.
\end{gathered}}

Anisotropy also makes #{O(Q)} compact. The proofs below deduce these
claims from the closed carriers and open Clifford quotient of
\ref{fcap-0045} and \ref{fcap-0046}, using the scalar-kernel and
reverse-norm identities of \ref{fcap-002D}. Thus compactness depends
on the quadratic form, even when the projection has finite kernel.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{If #{V=0}, the generated Spin carrier and both orthogonal groups
are singletons by \ref{fcap-0045}; all assertions follow. Suppose
henceforth that #{\dim_K V>0}, and let #{C\subseteq O(Q)} be compact.
Write #{\tau:\Gamma(Q)\to O(Q)} for the open surjection in
\ref{fcap-0046}. Choose a lift of each point of #{C}, and an open
neighborhood of that lift with compact closure in the locally compact
group #{\Gamma(Q)}. Their open images cover #{C}. A finite subcover
therefore supplies a compact set #{L\subseteq\Gamma(Q)} such that
#{C\subseteq\tau(L)}. If #{C} is empty, its preimage is already compact.}

\p{Let #{x\in\Spin(Q)} have action in #{C}. Choose #{y\in L} with
#{\tau(y)=\rho(x)}, viewing #{SO(Q)} inside #{O(Q)}. The scalar-kernel
identity gives #{x=cy} for some #{c\in K^\times}. The star-unitary
Spin convention has reverse norm one on even elements by
\ref{fcap-0028}. Hence the scalar-scaling identity of \ref{fcap-002D}
gives

##{\begin{gathered}
1=c^2N_{\mathrm{rev}}(y),\\
c^2=N_{\mathrm{rev}}(y)^{-1}.
\end{gathered}}

The inverse norm is continuous on #{L} by \ref{fcap-0046}. Its
absolute value is bounded there, say by #{M\geq1}, so #{|c|\leq\sqrt M}.
Closed bounded scalar balls are compact over a local field: in the
nonarchimedean case they are closed subsets of a scalar dilate of the
compact valuation ring, and in the archimedean cases this is the usual
closed-ball compactness. Write #{D=\{a\in K:|a|\leq\sqrt M\}}.
Then the set

##{DL=\{ay:a\in D,\ y\in L\}}

is compact in #{\Cl(Q)}, as a continuous image of a product of compact sets. The
preimage of #{C} in Spin is closed in the Clifford algebra: Spin is
closed by \ref{fcap-0045}, #{C} is closed in the Hausdorff group
#{O(Q)}, and the projection is continuous. It is contained in the
displayed compact set, so it is compact. A compact subset of #{SO(Q)}
is also compact in #{O(Q)}, proving properness of #{\rho}.}

\p{Suppose now that #{Q} is anisotropic. Choose coordinates and put
#{\|v\|=\max_i|v_i|}. The unit sphere #{\|v\|=1} is a compact
subset of the coordinate unit ball. On it the continuous function
#{|Q|} is positive, so has a positive minimum #{m}. For #{v\ne0},
choose a coordinate #{a=v_j} of maximal absolute value. Then
#{\|a^{-1}v\|=1}, giving

##{\begin{aligned}
|Q(v)|&=|a|^2|Q(a^{-1}v)|\\
&\geq m\|v\|^2.
\end{aligned}}

This also holds at zero. If #{g\in O(Q)}, its #{i}-th column satisfies
#{Q(ge_i)=Q(e_i)}, so the inequality bounds every column, uniformly
in #{g}. The closed matrix carrier #{O(Q)} from \ref{fcap-0045}
is consequently a closed subset of a compact finite product of scalar
balls. Thus #{O(Q)} is compact. Its inverse image is all of Spin,
which is compact by the properness just proved.}

\p{Conversely, suppose #{Q} is isotropic. Take #{u\ne0} with
#{Q(u)=0}. Write #{B_Q} for the unhalved polar form of
\ref{fcap-0027}. Nondegeneracy supplies #{w} with #{B_Q(u,w)=1}; set
#{v=w-Q(w)u}. Then #{Q(v)=0} and #{B_Q(u,v)=1}. Such #{u,v} form a
hyperbolic pair: their plane #{H=Ku+Kv} has
#{Q(au+bv)=ab}, and is nondegenerate, so #{V=H\perp H^\perp}.
This is the hyperbolic plane of
\citet{I.3, Theorems 3.2 and 3.4, pp. 9--10}{lam2005quadratic};
Lam's symmetric bilinear form is our half-polar form #{b_Q=B_Q/2}.
It specializes to the real model in \ref{fcap-0012}.}

\p{For #{t\in K^\times}, define #{h_t} by

##{\begin{gathered}
h_tu=tu,\quad h_tv=t^{-1}v,\\
h_t|_{H^\perp}=\operatorname{id}.
\end{gathered}}

It preserves #{Q} and has determinant #{1}. Square classes have
exponent two, so #{\nu_Q(h_t^2)=\nu_Q(h_t)^2=1}. The exact image
statement \ref{fcap-0023} therefore gives an actual Spin lift of each
#{h_t^2}. Choose #{a\in K^\times} with #{|a|>1} and let #{t=a^r},
#{r=1,2,\ldots}. In a basis beginning with #{u,v}, the first diagonal
entry of #{h_t^2} is #{a^{2r}}, an unbounded scalar sequence. If Spin
were compact, its continuous matrix image and every matrix coordinate
would be compact, hence bounded. This contradiction proves noncompactness,
including when #{H=V} is a split binary plane.}
}

\p{TauCeti formalizes properness and the compactness criterion in
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.isProperMap_spinToSpecialOrthogonal}
and \leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.isCompact_spinGroup_iff}.
Their hypotheses are a weakly locally compact nontrivially normed field,
finite dimension, invertible two and nondegeneracy. The supporting
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.isCompact_orthogonalGroup}
assumes anisotropy and finite dimension over the same kind of field,
without a separate nondegeneracy or characteristic condition. These
statements use the canonical module and subgroup topologies; on the
locally compact Hausdorff targets here, formal properness agrees with the
compact-preimage condition used above.}
